    "slug": "outsourcedhelpdeskservices-audit-saved-replies-after-a-product-rename",
    "title": "Audit saved replies after a product rename",
    "excerpt": "A product rename hides stale text in macros, signatures, links, and personal shortcuts, so the audit has to reach every surface an agent can insert.",
    "service": "helpdesk-operations",
    "minutes": 8,
    "published": "2026-11-19",
    "sources": [
          {"name": "NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security and privacy controls", "url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final"},
          {"name": "Google Workspace administrator help: manage saved replies", "url": "https://support.google.com/a/answer/9429221"},
          {"name": "NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},
          {"name": "ISO 18295-1:2017 customer contact centres", "url": "https://www.iso.org/standard/70677.html"}
        ],
    "body": [
          "## Build a list of every reply surface",
          "A product rename reaches the help desk through marketing, but it lands in saved replies, macros, signatures, and knowledge articles that nobody updated. Start the audit by listing every place an agent can insert prepared text, including the ticketing system, the chat tool, the email client, and the shared drive. Add the templates the client sends directly, because those often bypass the support team's review. The list is the scope of the audit and should be finished before anyone edits a single reply.",
          "Give each surface an owner and a last-reviewed date. A surface without an owner will be missed in the next rename, no matter how thorough this audit is. Include the export or backup path, because a bulk edit that cannot be reversed is a risk of its own. The owner should confirm the list rather than receive it as a surprise.",
          "## Search for the old name, not only the new one",
          "Search for the old product name, the old abbreviation, and the old domain in every surface, including the parts that are not visible to customers. Agents often paste the old name into internal notes, and those notes become the source for the next reply. Search for the new name as well, because a partial update can leave one reply using both names in the same sentence. Save the search results as evidence of what was found and what was changed.",
          "Do not replace every occurrence with blind find-and-replace. Some mentions refer to a historical product, a legal entity, or a migration path that customers still need. Mark those cases and decide each one with the product owner. A reply that erases a real distinction will confuse the customer who is still on the old plan, and the agent will have no way to explain the difference.",
          "## Check links, attachments, and screenshots",
          "Open every link in the saved reply and confirm where it lands. A renamed product often leaves redirects that work for a while, then fail when the old page is removed. Check attachments for screenshots that still show the old interface, since an agent may send one without reading the text. Replace the image or remove the attachment, and record which version is current.",
          "Review the signature block and the legal footer as separate items. These are easy to overlook because they sit outside the body an agent edits. A wrong legal name can matter more than a wrong product name in a regulated industry. Ask the client's legal or compliance contact to approve any change to the entity name before it goes live.",
          "## Test the reply against real customer wording",
          "Ask three agents to use the revised reply on a test ticket written in everyday customer language. A reply can be accurate and still fail because the opening does not match the question. Watch where the agent pauses or edits the text, since those moments show which sentence is unclear. Keep the test tickets with the audit record so the next reviewer can repeat the check.",
          "Include one case where the customer uses the old name and one where the customer uses a nickname. Customers rarely adopt a new name on the announcement date, and an agent needs a polite way to answer without correcting them. Write that sentence once and place it in the reply. Consistency across agents matters more than a perfect script.",
          "## Retire stale replies instead of hiding them",
          "When a reply is out of date, retire it rather than leaving it visible with a warning label. Agents under pressure use what is easiest to find, and a warning is easy to ignore. Move the old reply to an archive that requires a deliberate search, and keep a short note explaining why it was removed. The archive protects the history without competing with the current answer.",
          "Check that the retired reply no longer appears in favourites, shortcuts, or personal templates. A shared audit can pass while an agent's saved shortcut still inserts the old text. Ask each agent to clear personal shortcuts at the same time, and spot-check a few afterwards. The cleanup is finished only when the old text is hard to reach.",
          "## Set an expiry review for renamed products",
          "Set a review date tied to the product lifecycle, not to the calendar quarter. The next rename, packaging change, or pricing update should trigger the same audit, and the trigger should be written into the client agreement. Assign a named reviewer and a maximum number of days to complete the check. A trigger without an owner becomes a suggestion.",
          "Record the audit in one page: surfaces checked, changes made, cases held for review, and the date of the next trigger. Give a copy to the client's product contact and keep one with the support team. That record answers the first question a customer asks when something looks wrong, which is whether anyone checked before the change. A short, dated page is stronger evidence than a long retrospective.",
          "## Plan the switch for a low-volume window",
          "Schedule the replacement during a period when the queue is usually quiet, and keep the old reply available to a small group until the new one is confirmed. The group should include the team lead and one experienced agent who can answer questions from the floor. Announce the window in the shift notes and the client update. A quiet switch gives the team room to fix mistakes before customers see them.",
          "After the switch, read the first twenty replies that use the new text and compare them with the question each customer asked. Look for replies that are technically current but no longer answer the original problem. Add the mismatch to the audit record and correct the reply once, centrally. Individual coaching will not fix a template that asks the wrong question."
        ],
