    "slug": "outsourcedhelpdeskservices-plan-support-coverage-for-a-seasonal-promotion",
    "title": "Plan support coverage for a seasonal promotion",
    "excerpt": "A seasonal promotion changes both the volume and the mix of support requests, so coverage planning starts with the offer and the questions it creates.",
    "service": "helpdesk-operations",
    "minutes": 9,
    "published": "2026-11-19",
    "sources": [
          {"name": "NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security and privacy controls", "url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final"},
          {"name": "ISO 18295-1:2017 customer contact centres", "url": "https://www.iso.org/standard/70677.html"},
          {"name": "BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: customer service representatives", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},
          {"name": "NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}
        ],
    "body": [
          "## Estimate the request mix before the volume",
          "A seasonal promotion changes the support queue twice: volume rises, and the mix of questions changes. Start with the offer itself, then list the questions a customer must answer before they can buy. A discount that requires a code will produce code questions, while a free trial will produce eligibility and cancellation questions. The list is a better planning input than last year's total ticket count.",
          "Ask the marketing owner for the start date, the end date, and the exact wording customers will see. Small differences in wording create different support requests, so the desk should review the final copy before it publishes. Note any term that has no defined answer, such as whether the offer stacks with another discount. Those gaps become the first knowledge articles.",
          "## Separate new questions from repeat questions",
          "Split the forecast into questions new customers ask and questions existing customers ask. New customers need help finding the offer and completing a first order, while existing customers ask how the promotion affects a current plan. The two groups need different replies and often different queues. Combining them hides which team is under pressure when volume rises.",
          "Use the previous promotion's categories as a starting point, then adjust for changes in the offer. If the discount is larger or the eligibility is narrower, expect a different mix rather than the same total. Write the assumptions beside the forecast so the team can compare them with the first day's actuals. A forecast without assumptions cannot be corrected.",
          "## Set a temporary routing rule",
          "Create a temporary route for promotion questions that keeps them out of the general queue during peak hours. The route should have its own opening hours, its own saved replies, and one named owner. Do not send promotion questions to a queue that also handles outages or security reports, because the urgent work will be buried. A separate path lets the team measure the promotion without distorting the core service.",
          "Set a rule for when a promotion ticket becomes a normal support ticket. A code that fails because of a system fault is a technical case, not a marketing question. Write the handoff trigger and the information the next team needs. Clear boundaries prevent the temporary queue from becoming a place where difficult cases are parked.",
          "## Prepare knowledge and macros in advance",
          "Draft the answers before the first customer asks, and have the client approve them in writing. Cover eligibility, the discount amount, the deadline, the refund path, and what happens if the code fails. Each answer should say what the agent can do and what must be escalated. An approved answer is faster than a perfect answer written during the rush.",
          "Put the answers where agents already work, not in a separate promotion folder that nobody opens. Link them from the promotion queue and add the same links to the general queue during the campaign. Test the search terms a customer would use, including misspellings of the offer name. If the article cannot be found in ten seconds, it is not ready.",
          "## Protect the rest of the queue",
          "Reserve capacity for the customers who are not part of the promotion. A seasonal campaign can consume the attention of the whole desk, and the backlog appears after the promotion ends. Set a daily floor for ordinary requests and review it at the same time as the promotion numbers. The floor should be visible to the client, not a private promise.",
          "Watch the age of the oldest ordinary ticket rather than only the average. Averages stay healthy while a few cases wait for days, and those cases are the ones customers remember. Assign one person to review the oldest ten each morning and move them forward. The promotion is not a reason to let the core queue rot.",
          "## Agree on the refund and cancellation path",
          "A promotion that increases purchases also increases cancellations, and the desk needs a path that does not depend on a manager being available. Agree on who can approve a refund, what evidence is required, and how long the customer should wait. Put the threshold in the temporary queue so an agent can answer without transferring the call. The customer should hear the same rule from every agent.",
          "Separate a cancellation caused by the promotion from a cancellation caused by a product problem. The first is a policy question, while the second may need a technical review and a root-cause note. Record the reason with a fixed set of options so the post-promotion review can count them. Free-text reasons are hard to compare and easy to misread.",
          "## Brief the client on the trade-offs",
          "Tell the client what the desk will stop doing to absorb the promotion, such as delaying non-urgent quality reviews or pausing a knowledge cleanup. A trade-off that is not named looks like a failure when the ordinary work slows down. Put the trade-off in the weekly note with the date it will be reversed. The client can then judge the choice instead of discovering it later.",
          "Agree on one signal that would justify adding staff or extending hours during the campaign. Use an observable condition, such as the oldest ticket age crossing a stated threshold for two days. A pre-agreed trigger avoids a rushed negotiation in the middle of the peak. It also gives the client a reason to decide early rather than after the backlog forms.",
          "## Review the promotion after it ends",
          "After the promotion, compare the actual question mix with the forecast and note where it differed. Read a sample of tickets from each category and check whether the approved answer matched the customer's situation. Keep the temporary queue open long enough to handle redemptions and refunds, which often arrive after the campaign ends. Closing it on the end date creates a second surge in the general queue.",
          "Write a short handover for the next promotion that names what worked, what confused customers, and which answer needs revision. Include the timing of the peak and the channel that absorbed it. The next planner should not have to rediscover the same pattern. A one-page note is enough if it is specific, dated, and shared with the marketing owner."
        ],
